WEBVTT

00:00:00.381 --> 00:00:02.857
<v><b>Speaker:</b></v> Here’s a solid gold piece of advice:

00:00:03.408 --> 00:00:07.397
be wary of anyone offering you solid gold pieces of advice.

00:00:07.875 --> 00:00:10.408
The friend who advises you to, say,

00:00:10.433 --> 00:00:14.858
stay in your relationship or leave your job may well be looking out for you,

00:00:14.883 --> 00:00:20.275
but she’s inescapably looking out for herself, too, whether she realizes it or not.

00:00:20.962 --> 00:00:26.593
Maybe she thinks her own marriage is not perfect, and that you, too, should accept a similar situation.

00:00:27.056 --> 00:00:31.805
Maybe she adores your company so much she could never recommend a career step

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:33.642
that might involve your leaving town.

00:00:34.233 --> 00:00:39.391
Moreover, research suggests that people will generally advise you to act more cautiously

00:00:39.416 --> 00:00:42.279
than they would act themselves in a similar situation

00:00:42.779 --> 00:00:45.252
– perhaps because they don’t want it on their conscience

00:00:45.277 --> 00:00:48.341
if you take a daring leap and fall flat on your face.

00:00:49.181 --> 00:00:53.294
There’s a happy flip side to this, though, for parents, teachers, managers,

00:00:53.598 --> 00:00:57.572
and anyone else who finds themselves in the position of needing to motivate others:

00:00:58.284 --> 00:01:03.447
far better than giving them advice is to give them the opportunity to give advice.

00:01:04.013 --> 00:01:09.877
That’s the conclusion of a new study by psychologists at the Universities of Chicago and Pennsylvania,

00:01:10.134 --> 00:01:15.264
who found that American middle-school pupils were much more enthusiastic about doing their homework

00:01:15.289 --> 00:01:21.478
after dispensing advice on the topic to younger children than after receiving advice from teachers.

00:01:22.107 --> 00:01:26.362
This motivational effect lasted weeks and was also observed among adults

00:01:26.665 --> 00:01:31.610
who were attempting to lose weight, save money, control their temper, or find a job.

00:01:32.277 --> 00:01:37.725
Teach a man to fish and he’ll know how to fish – but get him to teach others how to fish,

00:01:37.948 --> 00:01:40.353
and he might actually do some fishing.

00:01:41.321 --> 00:01:43.832
This result isn’t all that surprising, I suppose,

00:01:43.857 --> 00:01:47.904
when you consider how flattering it feels to be invited to give advice.

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:53.090
Faced with a challenge, we tend to assume we need to seek advice in order to

00:01:53.115 --> 00:01:57.584
obtain more knowledge about how to proceed; yet the truth, very often,

00:01:57.912 --> 00:02:02.878
is that we know exactly what we need to do – we just lack the confidence to do it.

00:02:03.568 --> 00:02:08.518
The act of giving advice reacquaints us with the knowledge we already possess,

00:02:08.565 --> 00:02:12.353
which instils confidence, which motivates action.

00:02:12.869 --> 00:02:17.244
In fact, there are few bigger compliments you can pay another person than to ask,

00:02:17.269 --> 00:02:20.166
preferably sincerely, for their advice.

00:02:20.679 --> 00:02:24.871
As Benjamin Franklin, politician and founding father of the USA,

00:02:24.896 --> 00:02:28.337
famously observed, to ingratiate yourself with someone,

00:02:28.796 --> 00:02:32.125
it’s better to ask for a favour than to perform one:

00:02:32.672 --> 00:02:38.695
the favour-doer will come to think of you as the decent and likeable sort for whom they do favours.

00:02:39.196 --> 00:02:45.114
The same surely applies to advice: rather than giving it, ask for it.